@node Text Org Markdown and code
@section Text@comma{} Org@comma{} Markdown and code

Everything in this manual works.

@node eww and other read-only buffers
@section eww and other read-only buffers

Selecting@comma{} copying@comma{} jumping and deselecting work.  Killing@comma{} replacing and
running a command stop with ``Buffer is read-only''@comma{} as they would for an
ordinary region (killing copies first).

On a link@comma{} @code{C-drag} selects.  A @code{C-click} that selects nothing still
opens the link in a new window@comma{} as usual.

@node EPUB books
@section EPUB books

In books opened with nov.el@comma{} selections are remembered for each
chapter and come back when you return to it.  Copying collects them
from all chapters@comma{} in reading order.  @code{C-g} deselects everything in every chapter.

@node PDFs (pdf-tools)
@section PDFs (pdf-tools)

@findex multi-region-pdf-mouse-set-region
@findex multi-region-pdf-mouse-rectangle

A PDF page is a picture@comma{} so there is no point to move: you select with
the mouse.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Mouse
@tab What it does
@item drag
@tab Start over with a new selection on the page
@item @code{C-drag}
@tab Add another selection
@item click
@tab Nothing: the selections stay
@item @code{M-drag}
@tab Select a picture area@comma{} such as a figure
@item @code{M-click}
@tab Deselect the picture area under the pointer
@end multitable

Selections are remembered for each page@comma{} and copying collects them from
all pages.  @code{C-g} deselects everything on every page.

Copy@comma{} deselect last@comma{} deselect all and bring back work as in text.
Kill@comma{} replace@comma{} run-command and next/previous don't apply; @code{C-w} copies
instead of killing.

Highlights follow @samp{pdf-view-selection-style}@comma{} so what you see is
exactly what is copied.

@node PDFs (pdf-canvas)
@section PDFs (pdf-canvas)

pdf-canvas keeps several selections itself@comma{} on any number of pages
(drag@comma{} then @code{C-drag}; @code{M-drag} for a picture area).  multi-region gives
them the same commands as in a pdf-tools buffer@comma{} and @code{C-g} remembers
what it deselects@comma{} so @code{C-c M /} can bring it back.

@node Options
@chapter Options

Change these with @code{M-x customize-group RET multi-region}@comma{} or with
@samp{setq} in your init file.

@table @asis
@item @samp{multi-region-separator}
What goes between copied pieces.  A
newline by default.
@item @samp{multi-region-keep-after-copy}
Keep the selections after copying.
Off by default.
@item @samp{multi-region-thing}
What @code{C-c M SPC} selects at point: @samp{word} (the
default)@comma{} @samp{symbol}@comma{} @samp{sentence}@comma{} @samp{line}@comma{} or anything @samp{thing-at-point}
knows.
@item @samp{multi-region-image-margin}
Width in pixels of the band around
selected pictures.  4 by default; 0 for none.
@item @samp{multi-region-prefix-key}
The key that leads to the commands.
@code{C-c M} by default; see @ref{Other keys, , Other keys}.
@item @samp{multi-region-face}
How selections look.  Like the region@comma{} by
default.
@end table

@menu
* Other keys::
@end menu

@node Other keys
@section Other keys

@vindex multi-region-prefix-key
@vindex multi-region-mode-map
@vindex multi-region-command-map

To use @code{C-c s} as the prefix instead of @code{C-c M}:

@lisp
(setopt multi-region-prefix-key "C-c s")
@end lisp

@samp{setopt} needs Emacs 29.  On older versions@comma{} use @code{M-x customize-option
RET multi-region-prefix-key}@comma{} or @samp{setq} it @emph{before} multi-region is
loaded; a @samp{setq} afterwards does nothing.  Set it to @samp{nil} for no
prefix at all@comma{} and bind the commands in @samp{multi-region-command-map} to
keys of your own.

To select with @code{M-S-drag} instead of @code{C-drag}:

@lisp
(define-key multi-region-mode-map [C-down-mouse-1] nil)
(define-key multi-region-mode-map [M-S-down-mouse-1] #'multi-region-mouse-drag)
@end lisp

@node For other packages
@chapter For other packages

@findex multi-region-selections

@samp{(multi-region-selections &optional absorb)} returns the selections of
the current buffer in reading order@comma{} without changing them.  With
@samp{absorb}@comma{} an active region becomes a selection first.  Each selection is
a property list:

@itemize
@item
in text: @samp{(:beg BEG :end END :text TEXT)};
@item
in an EPUB@comma{} also @samp{:chapter}@comma{} the chapter number;
@item
with pictures@comma{} also @samp{:images}@comma{} each @samp{(:beg BEG :end END :file FILE)}@comma{}
or @samp{:data} and @samp{:type} for a picture that is not in a file;
@item
in a PDF: @samp{(:page PAGE :edges (LEFT TOP RIGHT BOTTOM) :text TEXT)}@comma{}
edges as fractions of the page.  A picture area also has @samp{:figure t}
and empty text.
@end itemize

@samp{(multi-region-clear)} deselects everything.

@node When pdf-tools is updated
@chapter When pdf-tools is updated

multi-region relies on a few details of how pdf-tools works inside.
They are all in one file@comma{} @samp{multi-region-pdf-tools.el}@comma{} listed at its
top.  After updating pdf-tools@comma{} run this in multi-region's folder:

@example
make test-pdf-tools
@end example

If something no longer holds@comma{} the test says which@comma{} for example ``breaks
assumption 2 in multi-region-pdf-tools.el''.  Until it is fixed@comma{} PDF
selections may not draw or copy correctly; text and EPUB buffers are
not affected.

